## Exploring the possibilities of increasing energy density and efficiency in rechargeable batteries
Download this complimentary article collection
The exponential rise in the need for better, more efficient power sources has sparked an incredible amount of research into batteries. A primary focus of research has been increasing the energy density of batteries, as it allows for lighter, more portable storage of energy. Lithium-ion batteries, for example, have a much higher energy density than conventional lead-acid batteries and can be used for various purposes, such as in electric vehicles.
## Defect Engineering of MOF-Based Membrane for Gas Separation
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are highly versatile materials that have been identified as promising candidates for membrane-based gas separation applications due to their uniformly narrow pore windows and virtually unlimited structural and chemical features. Defect engineering of MOFs has opened new opportunities for manipulating MOF structures, providing a simple yet efficient approach for enhancing membrane separation. However, the utilization of this strategy to tailor membrane microstructures and enhance separation performance is still in its infancy. Thus, this summary aims to provide a guideline for tailoring defective MOF-based membranes. Recent developments in defect engineering of MOF-based membranes will be discussed, including the synthesis strategies for defective MOFs, the effects of defects on the gas adsorption properties, gas transport mechanisms, and recently reported defective MOF-based membranes. Furthermore, the emerging challenges and future prospects will be outlined. Overall, defect engineering offers an exciting opportunity to improve the performance of MOF-based gas membranes. However, there is still a long way to go to fully understand the influence of defects on MOF properties and optimize the design of MOF-based membranes for specific gas separation applications. Nonetheless, continued research in this field holds great promise for the development of next-generation membrane-based gas separation technologies.
## 1. Introduction
Gas separation plays a crucial role in the chemical engineering industry, with three of the seven chemical separations that Sholl and Lively identified as essential for changing the world being related to gas separation.[1] Gas separation is essential for energy supply, environmental remediation, and everyday life, including the purification of chemical building blocks for plastics, the production of clean fuels for combustion, and carbon capture to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.[2] Cryogenic distillation, adsorption, and chemical absorption are currently the dominant techniques for gas purification in the industry, despite their energy-intensive or environmentally unfriendly nature.[3] However, membrane separation is emerging as a potential replacement candidate due to its advantages, such as ease of operation, low energy consumption, cost-efficiency, small footprint, and ease-of-integration.[4] MOFs, with uniformly tunable pore sizes and tailorable pore structures, possess the precise molecule sieving capability, and thus have been intensively employed for
the construction of both pure MOF membranes and mixed-matrix membranes (MMMs).[5] Due to their fixed sub-nanoscale porosity and specific topologies, MOF-based membranes are particularly suitable for the separation of gas mixtures with similar molecule sizes.[6] In the past decade, MOF-based gas separation membranes have witnessed substantial progress, pushing their performance beyond the commonly referred to Robeson upper bound line.[6g,7] However, their performance still lags behind the theoretical value due to two remaining challenges: precise adjustment of pore size at the sub-angstrom level and the fabrication of defect-free MOF-based membranes.[8]
Traditionally, the synthesis of perfect MOF crystals is pursued to fully exploit their potential for the fabrication of high-performance gas separation membranes.[9] However, it has been found that defective MOFs are more prevalent in the general synthesis process.[10] Surprisingly, defective MOFs frequently exhibit unexpected high performance in diverse applications, for example, adsorption, catalysis, electronics, magnetics, and so on, attributed to the enhanced porosity and the creation of abundant active sites.[11] As a result, defective MOFs provide a flexible platform and an additional dimensionality beyond compositional
variation and structure perfection to tailor the MOF structures.[12] Given their enhanced gas adsorption and heterogeneous catalysis, it can be anticipated that significantly improved gas separation performance could be achieved by using defective MOFs for membrane construction. Despite the potentially prominent separation performance, few reviews have mentioned the application of defective MOFs in gas separation membranes.[13] Therefore, it is crucial to summarize and provide future perspectives on the fabrication of MOF-based membranes via defect engineering, which will drive the gas separation membrane to a new level as an emerging field.
This review will concisely summarize recent developments in defective-MOF-based membranes for gas separation and provide guidelines on tailoring defective MOF structures for constructing gas separation membranes with precise molecule-sieving and gas permeability (Figure 1). The review will initially discuss the currently adopted approaches to the synthesis and characterization of defective MOFs, with a particular emphasis on the modulation of pore structures, including aperture size manipulation and pore modification. The basic gas transport theory for MOF-based membranes will then be illustrated to provide a deeper understanding of the structure–property relationships of defective-MOF-based membranes for separation, guiding the design and preparation of suitable defective MOFs. Subsequently, recent progress on the fabrication of gas separation membranes using defective MOFs will be detailed, and the role of defective MOFs will be discussed. Finally, future opportunities and remaining challenges in this emerging field will be presented.
## 2. Defect Types in MOF-Based Membranes
Defects in MOF-based membranes can be classified based on their location as either intracrystalline or intracrystalline defects. Intercrystalline defects, which include pinhole defects and grain boundary defects, adversely affect separation performance. On the other hand, intracrystalline defects, such as missing linker
and missing node defects, can actually enhance separation capabilities within the MOF framework. Consequently, this review will focus on harnessing intracrystalline defects as a strategy to boost the performance of MOF-based membranes in gas separation applications.
## 2.1. Intercrystalline Defects
Intercrystalline defects, such as mesopores, grain boundary defects, pinholes, and other cracks, are mesoscopic defects that form between crystals, creating non-selective channels within the membrane. In gas separation membranes, gas molecules need to be energetically activated to overcome the higher activation energy barrier from pores during mass transfer. Smaller pore sizes demand higher energy, and as a result, gas molecules preferentially transport through mesoscopic defects in the membrane due to their lower activation energy requirements.[15] Consequently, intercrystalline defects are undesirable in MOF-based membranes, as they compromise selectivity. Researchers continuously seek strategies to prevent the generation of these defects and strive to create defect-free membranes in order to achieve high-performance MOF-based membranes.[16]
## 2.2. Intracrystalline Defects
Intracrystalline defects are an inherent feature of MOF frameworks, making it nearly impossible to obtain MOF crystal structures without any defects, even under carefully controlled synthesis conditions. As defined by Fischer and his colleagues, intracrystalline defects are "sites that locally break the regular periodic arrangement of atoms or ions of the crystalline parent framework due to missing or dislocated atoms or ions".[17] The most common defects in the framework are point defects and extended defects. These defects can alter the framework's microstructure and ultimately impact separation characteristics by enhancing gas adsorption and intensifying mass transfer.[18] Owing to the unique properties of intracrystalline defects, the deliberate incorporation of defects into MOFs has emerged as a convenient approach for improving membrane performance. This strategy will be described and discussed in greater detail in Section 6.
## 3. Synthesis and Characterization of Defective MOFs
Defective MOFs can be purposely prepared and tailored by controlling the synthesis conditions. Numerous studies have been reported on constructing defects in MOF crystals, and the synthetic approaches can be broadly categorized into two types: in situ synthesis and post-synthetic modification. A schematic representation of the main approaches for forming defective MOFs is given in Figure 2. In this section, we will provide an overview and discussion of recent advances in the synthesis of defective MOFs, with a particular focus on the synthesis principles and how microstructural variations affect the properties of defective MOFs, such as BET surface area, pore size, and pore volume.
## 3.1. In Situ Synthesis Strategy
In situ synthesis strategy is a method for the construction of defective MOF crystals during the preparation process. As such, defects can be formed by controlling the synthesis conditions, which can hinder the perfect MOF crystal structure generation. Now, several methods have been proposed for in situ synthesis of defective MOFs, including modulator addition, mixed linkers, solvent regulation, and others.
## 3.1.1. Modulator Addition
MOFs are synthesized by connecting metal clusters and corresponding organic linkers via strong coordination bonds, obtaining ordered porous crystal structures. Thus, it can be anticipated that part of the crystal microstructures can be broken if monofunction linkers are added during the synthesis process, that is the formation of defective MOFs.[19] For example, defective UiO-66 can be obtained by quantitively blending the so called "modulators", such as trifluoro acetic acid (TFA), formic acid (FA), and difluoro acetic acid (DFA), into the precursor solutions to compete with the terephthalic acid (BDC) linkers for coordination with Zr clusters, resulting in missing linker defects.[20]
Modulator addition was initially adopted by Ravon et al. to prepare defective MOF-5 by using 2-methyl-toluic acid as the modulator during the synthesis process.[21] The formation of defects was confirmed by \(^1\mathrm{H}\) nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) and diffuse reflectance infrared Fouier transform spectroscopy (DRIFT): dicarboxylate was partially replaced by the monocarboxylate linker revealed by the liquid \(^1\mathrm{H}\) NMR measurements, and the appearance of a hydroxyl group originated from the modulator in the DRIFT spectra.
Later, the strategy was broadly applied to other defective MOF fabrications.[22] Among all fabrications, defective UiO-66 was the most studied one and has gained in-depth understanding of their structures. Yuan et al. applied the modulator approach to produce highly defective UiO-66 by using high-concentration benzoic acid as the modulator.[23] The number of BDC linkers in each cluster decreased from 6 to 4.4 with increasing the benzoic acid concentration, which resultantly boosted the BET surface areas, pore volumes, and pore size of UiO-66 crystals. Subsequently, Shearer et al. tailored the defects of UiO-66 by using different modulators, such as TFA, DFA, FA, and acetic acid (AA), and studied the BET specific surface area of UiO-66 with different numbers of defects (Figure 3a).[24] They found that defective UiO-66 synthesized by adding TFA as the modulator offered the largest BET surface \((1777\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and thus the biggest \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptake capacity (Figure 3b,c), arising from the stronger acidity of the modulator, rendering more defects in UiO-66. Moreover, the pore density variation (quantitatively described by the BET surface area) followed the same pattern as the defect numbers. With its distinct tunability, defective UiO-66 was employed for gas separation membrane fabrication in 2021 by using TFA as the modulator,[25] which realized enhanced \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) separation performance due to the increase in porosity (a more detailed discussion will be given in Section 6).
