## 3.1.2. Mixed Linker Approach
Contrary to the addition of modulators that has no contribution to crystal formation, the mixed linker approach, alternatively, provides organic linkers that competitively coordinate with metal clusters to form different MOF crystal structures, which thus results in the formation of defects due to the unmatched crystal structures induced by the two kinds of linkers.[27] More importantly, in comparison to the modulator approach, the removal of linkers is not required by using the mixed linker strategy owing to permanent coordination of the linker in the MOF crystals. For instance, the mixed linker strategy was used for the synthesis of defective HKUST-1 (Figure 3d).[26] In this work, different ratios of mixed linkers (trimesic acid (BTC) and isophthalic acid (F1)) and \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) were adopted to prepare a series of HKUST-1-F1. The \(^1\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectroscopy characterization disclosed that F1 could be successfully incorporated into the final HKUST-1-F1, leading to defects in the MOF crystals. As such, both the BE1 surface area and pore volume of HKUST-1-F1 were enlarged (Figure 3e), compared with pristine HKUST-1 crystal, arising from the missing linkers or missing metal cluster defects.
## 3.1.3. Other Methods
Besides the linker tuning, other factors, such as metal source, solvent types, and synthesis conditions, are also key factors for tailoring the defects in MOF crystals, which are also explored for the synthesis of defective MOFs. Yan et al. proposed utilizing \(\mathrm{ZrS}_{2}\) as the metal source to replace the commonly used \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) for the preparation of UiO-66 membranes.[28] Due to the lower homogeneous nucleation rate caused by \(\mathrm{ZrS}_{2}\), larger number of missing linker defects were introduced in the MOF framework, leading to enhanced separation performance.
Chaemchuen et al. investigate the synthesis condition effect on the formation of defects in ZIF-8 crystals by varying the synthesis methods, including solvothermal (SV), microwave-assistant (MW), spray drying (SP), and room temperature (RT) synthesis.[29] The characterizations demonstrated that all these ZIF-8 crystals were isostructural with the simulated pattern, but exhibited different crystal morphologies. The synthesized ZIF-8 was applied for catalytic reaction, and the catalytic performance was displayed in the order of \(\mathrm{SP}>\mathrm{MW}>\mathrm{RT}>\mathrm{SV}\), which can be correlated to the number of defects in ZIF-8 crystals. The author ascribed the disparity to the distinctive crystallization rate of ZIF-8 under various conditions. Therefore, the SP method with the highest crystallization rate led to highly defective ZIF-8, whereas the SV method with the lowest crystallization rate gave less defective ZIF-8.
## 3.2. Post-Synthetic Modification
Though in situ synthesis strategy proffering convenience for obtaining defective MOFs, some MOF crystals cannot be engineered with this method. Alternatively, post-synthetic modification has proven to be an efficient approach to introducing defects into the parent MOFs.[30] According to the post treatment conditions, the approach can be further divided into two ways, that is, physical treatment and chemical treatment.
## 3.2.1. Physical Treatment
Physical treatment is the utilization of additional energy, for example, ball-milling or thermal treatment, to create defects in the ordered MOF crystals.[30c,31] Bueken et al. proposed to produce defects in MOF crystals by breaking a fraction of metal–ligand bonding in the UiO-66, MIL-140B, and MIL-140C upon ball-milling.[32] The results showed that the inorganic clusters of UiO-66, \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\), remained intact when the structure collapsed, with the ZrO backbone of the MIL-140 frameworks being severely distorted. The reason is that the MIL-140 frameworks have fewer Zr–O bonds than that of UiO-66.
In another study, thermal annealing was used to create tiny defects in the ZIF-8 framework.[33] The defects were created by the transformation of pyridinic-N into pyrrolic-N and quaternary nitrogen with heating energy, which resulted in the Zn–N bond breaking in the ZIF-8 framework (Figure 4a). The defective ZIF-8, thus, possessed an enlarged surface area and pore volume, rendering more active sites for the adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (Figure 4b). Nevertheless, it should be mentioned that the annealing temperature and annealing time should be controlled to avoid broken ZIF-8 crystalline structures.
