## 3.2.2. Chemical Treatment
Chemical treatment works in a similar way to physical treatment, which also partially destroys the crystal structure of MOFs but relies on the chemical reaction process. For instance, Szilágyi et al. immersed the ordered MIL-101 (Cr) framework into \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-BDC linker solutions to manufacture defective MIL-101 crystals via solvent-assisted linker exchange (SALE), taking advantage of the fabulous chemical reactivity of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-BDC\(^{2-}\) linker.[13a] As illustrated in Figure 4c, in post-synthetically linker-exchanged sample (PSM), there exists dangling linker defects, which would help increase \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) adsorption (Figure 4d).
## 3.3. Characterization of Defects in MOF Crystals
Characterization of defective MOFs is essential for understanding the microstructures of MOF frameworks and guiding the design and controllable synthesis of defective MOF crystals. This serves as a 'bridge' to establish the relationship between defective microstructures and separation performance for MOF-based membranes. In 2014, Cliffe et al. pioneered the characterization of defective UiO-66 structures using tools such as polycrystalline X-ray diffraction (PXRD), pair distribution function (PDF), and anomalous X-ray scattering.[26] Since then, a wide range of characterization tools have been developed and applied to confirm defects in MOF crystals.
PXRD is a common technique for qualitatively examining the ordered defective phase of Zr-MOFs, as X-ray diffraction
intensities are more sensitive to \(Z\mathbf{r}\) atoms than organic linkers. Broad peaks in the low-angle region indicate the formation of ordered missing-cluster defects. However, this technique is primarily applied to \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs, such as UiO-66 and its derivatives.
TGA is another widely used characterization technique that provides information on the existence and number of missinglinker defects in MOFs. Defects can be anticipated to cause significantly lower weight loss during thermal decomposition than the theoretical value. By calculating the weight difference between ideal and defective samples, the number of missing-linker defects can be roughly obtained.[34]
Defects alter the pore structure of MOF crystals, so measuring pore size, pore volume, and BET specific surface area is a direct way to confirm defects in MOF crystals. Typically, inert gas (e.g., \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and He) is used as a molecular probe to investigate MOF pore structures, and parameters such as BET specific surface area can be calculated using gas adsorption isotherms.[35] Defective MOFs tend to have a lower mass density, resulting in higher pore volumes and BET surface areas.[25,36]
Positron annihilation lifetime spectroscopy (PALS) is an intriguing technique for detecting defective MOF porosity using positrons as the probe. In MOFs, the main electron density is located on the framework, so the Ps lifetime is closely related to pore size.[23] NMR and Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) are standard tools for examining the chemical structures of defective MOFs. By tracing chemical variations using NMR or FTIR, defects can be analyzed and confirmed.[37]
Confocal fluorescence microscopy (CFM) is a powerful technique for 3D visualization of defects in MOF single crystals. De
fects, like uncoupled \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{II})\) centers as Lewis acid sites, can be captured using an acid-catalyzed furfuryl alcohol probe reaction in CFM. CFM images allow localization of fluorescence signals resulting from the furfuryl alcohol oligomerization reaction catalyzed by the Lewis acid site[38]
Although many techniques have been employed for characterizing defects in MOFs, most provide indirect evidence due to resolution limitations, which may lead to misleading information when using a single tool. Therefore, several characterizations are necessary to confirm defects in MOF crystals. Developing combined characterization techniques and exploring novel characterization methods will significantly advance understanding of defects in MOF frameworks, enabling controllable tailoring of MOF defects and improving the performance of MOF-based membranes.
## 4. Transport Theory for Defective MOF-Based Gas Separation Membranes
Defect engineering in MOFs is an emerging method for tailoring the properties of MOF frameworks, such as porosity, BET surface area, and chemical properties, which are thus promising materials for membrane-based gas separation. However, till now, there have been few researches pinpointing the mechanism of gas transport in defective MOF-based membranes. In this section, the sorption–diffusion model, a basic theory to describe the gas transport process in the membrane, will be first discussed. According to this theory, we will subsequently discuss the promoted transport of gas molecules in defective MOF-based membranes.
## 4.1. Sorption–Diffusion Model
Sorption–diffusion model is a well-established theory to describe the gas transport mechanisms for dense polymeric membranes.[39] In most cases, this model is also valid for clarification of the gas transport process in ultra-microporous material-based membranes, such as MOFs, due to the similar pore sizes as the free volume of the polymers.[40] The figures of merit to evaluate membrane separation performance are permeability and selectivity.
For a single gas permeating through a membrane, the permeability is defined as the flux normalized by membrane thickness and the pressure driving force, which is viewed as an intrinsic material property and thereby utilized to characterize the diffusion rate of gas across the membrane. As a derivative of Fick's law, permeability can be written as the product of the diffusion coefficient and solution coefficient, known as the sorption–diffusion model[41]:
\[
P=D\times S
\]
where \( P \) is the permeability, \( D \) is the diffusion coefficient \((\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\mathrm{S}^{-1})\) , and \( S \) is the sorption coefficient \((\mathrm{cm}^{3}\) (STP) \(\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) (polymer) \(\mathrm{cm\Hg^{-1}}\) ). \( D \) reflects the mass transfer speed of the gas molecules in the membrane, which relates to pore structure and penetrant size. \( S \) represents the partition of penetrants between the gas phase and the adsorbed phase in the membrane, which is very sensitive to the affinity between the penetrant and the membrane.
Selectivity is a central parameter to evaluate the ability of a membrane to separate mixed gases. When the downstream pressure is negligible relative to the upstream pressure, the ideal selectivity can be described by
\[
\alpha_{\mathrm{A/B}}=\frac{P_{\mathrm{A}}}{P_{\mathrm{B}}}=\left(\frac{D_{\mathrm{A}}}{D_{\mathrm{B}}}\right)\left(\frac{S_{\mathrm{A}}}{S_{\mathrm{B}}}\right)
\]
where \( A \) and \( B \) are the two kinds of gases across the membrane and \( D_{\mathrm{A}}/D_{\mathrm{B}} \) is the diffusivity selectivity, representing the competing diffusion rates. \( S_{\mathrm{A}}/S_{\mathrm{B}} \) is the sorption selectivity, demonstrating the sorption difference of the gases in the membrane.
In polymeric membranes, transient gaps are created by segmental packing and motion of polymer chains, resulting in the stochastic distribution of the penetrant-sized gaps, which cannot be controlled and thereby leads to decreased diffusion selectivity.[42] On the contrary, MOFs with uniform subnanometer apertures could afford the distinctive benefits of precisely separating mixed gas penetrants, which demand delicate tailoring of the pore aperture size.[9c,43]
## 4.2. Gas Sorption and Transport in Defective MOFs
According to the theory outlined above, gas molecule transport through defective MOF-based membranes can be broadly characterized by three consecutive steps: 1) adsorption on the external surface of the membrane, 2) transport within the pores, and 3) desorption from the membrane.[44] Steps 1 and 3 are thermodynamic processes, analogous to the adsorption and desorption processes, respectively. The sorption coefficient, a primary thermodynamic parameter, is governed by the gas adsorption amount and gas-phase equilibrium pressure. Consequently, defects in MOFs can enhance the sorption coefficient of the membrane, thereby increasing permeability, by altering the chemical properties.
