Step 2 is a dynamic process and serves as the rate-determining step in permeation due to its higher activation energy barrier. The smaller the pore size, the more energy is required for gas molecule transportation within the membrane, and vice versa. Therefore, defect engineering of MOF membranes, including refining pore structures at the sub-angstrom level (intensifying separation) and increasing porosity (enhancing mass transfer), is beneficial for gas separation.
From the above discussion, it can be inferred that the intrinsic impetus of defects in improving gas separation performance is increased gas adsorption capacity and accelerated gas transport. Hence, tailoring defects in the MOF framework through the incorporation of active adsorption sites and enlargement of pore windows is key to allowing more gas molecules to pass through, ultimately improving separation.[45]
## 5. Gas Adsorption
Since its initial appearance, MOFs were substantially adopted for gas adsorption, which is also an unavoidable but principal step for gas separation in membrane processes.[46] Defective MOFs have shown fascinating gas adsorption and separation capabilities due to their increased surface area, pore volume, and active sites, thereby significantly boosting their gas adsorption performance. Resultantly, according to the sorption–diffusion model, the gas separation performance of MOF membranes could be remarkably magnified via defect engineering of MOFs by concurrently strengthening the diffusion (surface area and pore volume enlargement) and solution (active sites increase) processes. In this regard, defect engineering of MOFs for gas adsorption is concisely illustrated and discussed here, which will provide meaningful experience and a basic guideline for the following defect engineering of MOF-based membranes.
UiO-66, famous for its distinguished stability, has been extensively exploited for structure tailoring via defect engineering. In a study, Jiao et al. prepared defective UiO-66 via a modified synthesis coupling post-synthetic heat strategy (Figure 5a), which markedly boosted the gravimetric BET surface area to \( 1747\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~cm}^{-3} \) (23% enhancement compared with pristine UiO-66 crystals).[47] With such-high surface area, the authors employed the defective UiO-66 for three kinds of gas or vapor adsorption, including \( \mathrm{SO}_{2} \), benzene, and cyclohexane. The result demonstrated that compared to pristine UiO-66, the adsorption capacity of defective UiO-66 exhibited 20%, 49%, and 167% enhancement for \( \mathrm{SO}_{2} \), benzene, and cyclohexane, respectively (Figure 5b). The variation is not consistent with the surface area enhancement, which was ascribed to the enlarged pore size and more active centers in the defective UiO-66 due to missing linking. Thus, it considerably promoted the adsorption capacity, particularly for the large size benzene and cyclohexane that cannot enter the pores of pristine UiO-66.
Pore size, in addition to pore volume and surface area, is a crucial factor for enhancing the gas adsorption capability of MOFs.
Ren et al. used formic acid as a modulator to create highly defective core–shell MIL101(Cr)@UiO-66(Zr), aiming to tune the pore size.[48] After a series of characterizations, they discovered that the pore size of the prepared defective MOFs increased with the synthesis period. The fabricated defective MOFs were tested for their gas sorption capacities at \( 77\,\mathrm{K} \) and 1 bar. When the synthesis period was below \( 24\,\mathrm{~h~} \), both \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) sorption capacities increased with synthesis time, resulting from the enlarged pore size due to missing-linker defects. However, beyond this period, the gas adsorption capacity declined despite the continued increase in pore size. This unexpected phenomenon occurred because when the pore size is too large compared to the tested gas molecules, the jump pathway is extended, reducing the collision rate (i.e., low adsorption) between the gas molecules and MOF materials.[49] Therefore, precise refinement of pore structure is essential for achieving high gas adsorption by strengthening the interaction between gas molecules and pore walls, which can be effectively accomplished through defect engineering strategies.
In addition to pore features, unsaturated metals in MOFs also significantly impact gas adsorption due to the electron-vacancy orbits facilitating their interaction with gas molecules. Consequently, numerous studies have deliberately manufactured defective MOFs with more unsaturated metal sites via defect engineering. Zhang and colleagues mixed benzoic acid (BA) into MOF-199 precursor solutions as the defect-introducing linker, obtaining BA-MOF-199 (defective MOF-199) with more unsaturated copper centers.[50] Moreover, the pore volume of BA-MOF-199 increased with BA concentration. Due to the combined effects, BA-MOF-199 exhibited superior \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S} \) and \( \mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{SCH}_{3} \) removal abilities, with sulfur breakthrough capacities of 69.2 and \( 78.9\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1} \), respectively, owing to the increased active sites (unsaturated copper centers) in BA-MOF-199 available for adsorption (Figure 6). Similarly, Fischer's group prepared defective Ru-MOF by replacing part of the parent linkers with ditopic isophthalate, yielding unsaturated metal centers in the defective Ru-MOF.[51] As expected, the defective Ru-MOF demonstrated enhanced adsorption capability for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \), CO, and \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \), stemming from the increased active Ru sites.
The above examples demonstrate that defect engineering can facilely and effectively alter the surface area, expose active sites, pore size, pore volume, and so on, which thus accelerates gas
adsorption capability. As discussed in Section 4, the enhanced gas adsorption contributes to the increase of sorption coefficient of the membrane, which will definitely promote the gas separation performance. However, it is still at the infant stage, and more and more effective methods in defect engineering of MOFs can be coined with judicious chemical design in the future. Therefore, it provides a versatile platform for improving the gas separation process in MOF membranes.
## 6. Gas Separation through Defect-Engineered MOF-Based Membranes
With the improved gas adsorption capability, defective MOFs have been employed for the construction of membranes for gas separation. Though still in the beginning, defect engineering of MOFs strategy delivers a new dimension for developing defective MOF-based membranes with excellent gas separation performance. In this section, we summarize recent progress on defective-MOF-based membranes for gas separation and emphasize the role of defective MOFs in the separation process. According to the components of the membranes, we divided the defective MOF-based membranes into pure defective MOF-based membranes and defective MOF-based MMM. Table 1 summarizes the reported defective-MOF-based membranes and their gas separation performance.
## 6.1. Pure Defective MOF Membranes
Owing to the uniform pore sizes, pure thin MOF membranes are always sought after, which is assumed to offer ultrahigh separation performance.[55] However, due to the hard-to-eliminate inter-crystalline defects, the resultant pure MOF membranes are commonly too thick, which thus hamstrings the gas mass transfer speed, that is, the permeability.[56a,b,56] With their enhanced pore volume and more adsorption sites, the pure defective-MOF-based membranes are promising for alleviation of the issue.
Qiao and co-workers developed ultrathin, low-crystallinity MOF membranes (45–150 nm) via the interface layer polarization induction methodology with abundant open metal sites.[52]
Schematic of the formation process is illustrated in Figure 7a. The constructed ZIF-8 membrane was endowed with large number of open metal sites due to the competitive complexation with \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) by poly (vinyl alcohol), which was confirmed with XPS measurements (Figure 7b). By deliberate addition of diethanolamine (DEA) in the organic linker solution, more defective ZIF-8 was obtained, arising from the DEA suppression of the effective coordination between organic linker and \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \), creating much more open metal sites. In virtue of \( \pi \) bond interactions between \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) and \( \mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6} \), the fabricated defective ZIF-8 membrane was adopted for \( \mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8} \) separation. Noteworthy, the membrane exhibited
the recorded \( \mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6} \) permeance of 2000–3000 GPU with an excellent \( \mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8} \) selectivity of 90–120, as shown in Figure 7c, arising from the more exposed \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) preferring interaction with \( \mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6} \).
With abundant active sites, the defective MOFs could also strengthen the interfacial interaction, which is essential to reduce the interfacial defects.[17,58] Kwon et al. synthesized an ultrathin ZIF-8 membrane via a defect-inducing Ostwald-ripening-like process by using the defective ZIF-8.[57] As shown in Figure 8a, using the microwave-assisted seeding technique, ZIF-8 nanocrystals (≈100 nm) with defects were deposited on a- \( \mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \)
support in a few minutes. Assisted with steam and ligand vapors', the ZIF-8 nanocrystal grew larger and gradually eliminated the intracrystalline gaps, at the expense of smaller ZIF-8 crystals in the ripening process due to the enhanced activity of defective ZIF-8 nanoparticles. The resultant defective ZIF-8-based membranes with a thickness of \({\approx}300{-}400\ \mathrm{nm}\) showed an enhanced \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) selectivity of \({\approx}1.20\) , with a propylene permeance of \(1.25\times10^{-8}\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\;\mathrm{Pa}^{-1}\) .
Recently, Liu's group prepared defective UiO-66-based membranes, taking advantage of their distinctive stability.[28] They fabricated a (111)-oriented UiO-66 membrane via structural defect engineering coupling oriented tertiary growth for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) separation. In their work, \(\mathrm{ZrS}_{2}\) was used as a metal source for the preparation of UiO-66 nanoparticles with solvothermal synthesis, which created a higher number of missing linkers within the framework. Resultantly, the created missing linker defects increased the BET surface area, pore volume, as well as more active sites, which thus enhanced the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capability. After tertiary growth, the defective UiO-66-based membrane showed superior \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeance of \(4.07\times10^{-7}\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\;\mathrm{Pa}^{-1}\) and a high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity of 35.6. However, the defective UiO-66 synthesis suffers from the high temperature limitation. Later, the same group developed a room-temperature preparation of defective UiO-66 strategy where the preorganized nodes, \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\) (metal-oxo cluster), were utilized as a zirconium source to reduce the activation energy and control the missing-linker number (Figure 9a).[53] They demonstrated that the synthesized UiO-66 exhibited higher defect densities, which could be precisely adjusted by changing the reaction temperature or organic-linker/Zr ratio. With the tertiary growth method, the fabricated defective UiO-66-based membrane offered exceptional \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity of 37.8, with a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeance of \(2.11\times10^{-8}\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\;\mathrm{Pa}^{-1}\) .
