By virtue of the metal-oxo cluster protocol, the group further fabricated missing-linker defective MIL-125-based membranes by using \(\mathrm{Ti}_{8}(\mu_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{8}(\mathrm{OOCC}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5})_{16}\) cluster as the Ti source.[14] As shown in Figure 8b, during the fabrication of the defective MIL-125-based membrane with tertiary growth, a single-mode microwave heating method was introduced to enable the formation of well-intergrown MIL-125 membranes and effective suppression of MIL-125 nucleation in the bulk solution. The final defective MIL-125-based membrane presented an enhanced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity of 38.7 (ranked the highest level among MOF membranes), with a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeance of \(1.24\times10^{-7}\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\;\mathrm{Pa}^{-1}\) (Figure 8c,d). The improved separation performance is attributed to the intensified interaction between the exposed Ti and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules.
## 6.2. Defective MOF-Based MMMs
Though pure MOF membranes deliver potentially high gas separation performance, the inter-crystal defects and hard-to-solution-processible features hinder the large-scale manufacture of MOF membranes for industrial applications.[59] Alternatively, MMMs, which combine the precise-molecule sieving merit of
MOFs with solution-processible ability of polymers, have received intensive attention and witnessed rapid progress in the past decade.[8b,60] However, the interfacial compatibility issue between MOF nanoparticles and polymer chains and the easy-to-aggregate problem of MOF particles still hampers the further development of MMMs.[16e,61] The emerging of defective MOFs proffers a versatile platform for intensifying the interaction
between MOF nanoparticles and polymer chains, which could mitigate the interfacial issue in the MMMs.
A well-formed interface between the MOF nanoparticles and polymer chains is critical for the fabrication of defect-free and mechanically stable MMMs, especially with high MOF loadings.[6f,62] By computational calculation, Maurin et al. demonstrated that engineering MOF surface defects in MMMs is an effective strategy to enhance MOF/polymer adhesion.[63] They simulated the dynamics of the interface between defective UiO-66 and Microporosity-1 (PIM-1) in the MMM and observed that additional interfacial pockets created by the defective MOF surface were formed to accommodate parts of the polymer chains. The physical immobilization effects contribute not only to improving the MOF/polymer interface adhesion but also to reducing the polymer chains mobility, which may help mitigate plasticization.
UiO-66 reaps a good reputation for its distinctive stability, which has been well studied for tailoring its structures and relevant characterizations.[10a,64] As such, Park's group conducted studies on manufacture of defective UiO-66-based MMM via defect engineering.[25] In their work, trifluoroacetic acid was used as a modulator to produce highly defective UiO-66 nanoparticles. The number of defects raised with modulator concentration was confirmed by XRD spectra. As a result, the surface area, pore volume, pore size, and open metal sites significantly increased, which was approved by a combination of several characterizations, including TGA, Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET), solid-state \( ^{19}\mathrm{F} \) NMR measurements, and gas sorption experiments. Afterward, the synthesized defective UiO-66 nanoparticles were doped into 6FDA-DAM, to form defect-engineered UiO-66/6FDA-DAM MMMs. The defective UiO-66 nanoparticles in the MMMs could concurrently serve as a fast diffusion pathway and proffer selective adsorption sites for \( C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6} \), owing to the Zr-olefin complexation, illustrated in Figure 10a. The MMM containing \( 40~\mathrm{wt\%} \) UiO-TF36 was thus employed for \( C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8} \) separation, reaching a \( C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6} \) permeability of 365 Barrer while maintaining a \( C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8} \) selectivity of 9.4 (Figure 10b). Besides, the MMM also displayed excellent long-term stability and aging resistance, attributed to the favorable interfacial interaction
between defective UiO-66 and the polymer matrix. Taking advantage of the enhanced gas adsorption capability, the above defective UiO-66 nanoparticles were added into a poly (ethylene glycol) diacrylate (PEGDA) matrix for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) separation.[54] Under the optimized conditions, the prepared MMM provided a boosted \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) permeability of 470 Barrer, fourfold enhancement compared to the pristine polymer membrane, with an unvaried \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) selectivity of 41. The high separation performance can be accounted by the good polymer-inorganic filler adhesion due to the open metal site function, which was also disclosed by the computer simulations.
ZIF-8, one of the most researched MOFs, has also been investigated for the fabrication of defective MMMs via defect engineering. Coudert et al. used an alkyl amine-induced defect engineering approach to fabricate defective ZIF-8 nanoparticles, where monodentate amine modulators were added to competitively coordinate \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) mental ions (Figure 11a).[37] In this way, the pore structure of the defective ZIF-8 was finely regulated, which played a central role in strengthening the interfacial interaction between MOF and polymer chains (Figure 11b,c). The defective ZIF-8 nanoparticles were blended with up to \( 50~\mathrm{wt\%} \) loading to prepare the defective ZIF-8-based MMMs, as shown in Figure 11d, which achieved significantly enhanced \( C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8} \) separation performance, that is, a \( C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6} \) permeability of 79.38 Barrer with a \( C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8} \) selectivity of 39.8 (3.6-fold higher than pure 6FDA-DAM).
In another study, Woy et al. developed thermal annealing post-treatment to create the tiny defects in the ZIF-8 framework.[33] The defects were produced by the broken \( \mathrm{Zn-N} \) bonds, and the pyridinic-N transformed into pyrrolo-N and quaternary nitrogen with thermal annealing treatments. Consequently, a large number of polar atoms appeared in the ZIF-8 framework, which is beneficial in promoting the affinity toward \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \).[65] The defective ZIF-8 nanoparticles were adopted to prepare MMM with a loading of \( 20\,\mathrm{wt\%} \). The MMM exhibited a \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) permeability of 39.74 and a \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) selectivity of 22, augmented 2.7- and 1.8-fold, respectively, compared with the pristine poly(styrene-co-butadiene) membrane, which was due to the polar atoms endowed by the
defective ZIF-8 nanoparticles.
In short, defect engineering of the MOFs furnishes a refreshed and versatile platform for tailoring the structures of MOFs, such as pore size, pore volume, surface area, open metal sites, etc., which are of significance for solving the issues of the current MOF-based membranes, for example, interfacial incompatibility, low MOF loadings, and so on. The emerging technique is recognized as a novel dimension for tuning membrane microstructures. At the infant stage, there is spacious room to exploit more functions in the defective MOFs to solve the dilemma faced in the membrane field.
## 7. Summary and Future Perspectives
Defect engineering of MOF-based separation membranes is an emerging field that has attracted increasing attention due to the distinguished features of defective MOFs, including enhanced surface area, tailorable pore architectures, exposed active sites, and solution-processing ability. This review summarizes recent advances in defect engineering of MOF-based membranes for gas separation, including approaches for synthesizing defective MOFs, the effects of defective MOF microstructure on gas adsorption performance, and the application of defect MOFs for gas
separation membrane preparation. Despite some achievements, the development of defective MOF-based gas separation membranes is still at an initial stage, with several challenges to be addressed for establishing structure–property relations to design well-structured membranes for targeted separation systems.
First, more efforts should be devoted to extending the types of defective MOFs for various gas separation membranes providing enough and forceful data for the establishment of structure–property relations between defective MOF-based membranes and separation performance. Second, controlled preparation of defect MOFs, that is, defect concentration, spatial distribution, and chemical properties, is of importance, and future research should focus on controlled synthesis methods combined with molecule structure simulations. Third, advanced characterization techniques are needed for detecting and analyzing local defects to understand their impacts on gas mass transfer processes. Lastly, industrial applications of MOF-based membranes have not been realized due to poor solution processability, but with defect engineering, it is possible to fabricate more pure MOF membranes and mixed matrix membranes via solution processing techniques.
It is important to note that most of the current research focuses on limited MOF types, such as UiO-66 and ZIF-8, and thus there
is limited data available for guidance on designing novel defective MOFs or membrane microstructures for enhanced separation performance. Moreover, there is a lack of knowledge on the microstructures of defective MOFs with current techniques, and therefore, future research should focus more on controlled synthesis methods combined with molecule structure simulations to offer an in-depth understanding of the mechanism of defect formation in MOF-based membranes. Additionally, advanced techniques or combined characterizations are urgently needed to access the defects of MOFs and deliver more information on the local microstructures,[66] and computational modeling is also imperative for predicting the microstructures of defective MOFs and corresponding membranes.
In conclusion, defect engineering of MOF-based separation membranes is a promising field with great potential for enhancing gas separation performance, but more research is needed to extend the types of defective MOFs, control their preparation, and advance the characterization techniques to understand their impacts on gas mass transfer processes. Ultimately, the goal is to achieve industrial application of MOF-based separation membranes, which can be realized by fabricating pure MOF membranes and mixed matrix membranes via solution processing techniques.
